            "review_text": "p911turbo\nTexas474 contributions\n0\nwent again and didn't like it this time - what happened?\nJul 2018 • Couples\nKubara is a fine dining Japanese restaurant. The dishes are not cheap in the local standard. However the services were not as efficient as those of Japanese food village (next door). The English level of waiters and waitresses is still below standards. They may be able to say 'Thank you very much' and 'Welcome' in Japanese perfectly but they could not even understand simple English orders - quite frustrating (especially you are hungry)! \n\nI went there again (and therefore writing another review again here) because I wanted something bland and simple. When we got there, we thought that we could have some peace as I saw other Japanese diners too. As times went by, the local came in and the restaurant became a food court. \n\nWhen we sat down, they automatically put down 2 glasses of iced barley tea. I meant, care to ask what we would like? Barley tea instead of green tea? Were we at a Korean BBQ again? Then I told her barley tea is fine but we don't want any ice politely. She brought me 1 tea with no ice then and left the 2 glasses of iced tea on the already small table. I had to repeat myself to get the other tea for my company. Asked her about the items on the menu. Not sure if she got it or not. Smiling is fine but let's be practical and answer my questions or get help. I saw one Japanese customer having a hard time with them too in a conversation.\n\nFood didn't came fast and didn't know why as the restaurant wasn't busy at all. When the mushroom stew with udon came (280000dong, still charged me the same even though I said we didn't want the rice roll with the tofu skin wrap), it smelled burnt and it tasted burnt. The udon was found stuck onto the bottom of the claypot. We were hungry, we closed our eyes and ate a bit. I liked the broth of my chicken stew with udon (thats why I gave it 3 stars). It was clear and free of grease but way too much cabbage and udon was not really there. \n\nThey need to walk around to check on the customers instead of mingling around.\nRead more\nValue\nService\nFood\nWritten August 1, 2018",
